       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 20 mg is double the FDA-labeled maximum of 10 mg once daily for adults. Higher doses add more edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia without much extra blood pressure lowering.\n\nCould this be a typo, or is there a reason for the higher dose? Here are some options:\n\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO daily**: the maximum standard dose.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO daily**: the usual starting dose, which you can titrate up after 1\u20132 weeks. Use 2.5 mg for frail or elderly patients or those with hepatic impairment.\n- **Add a second agent**: if 10 mg isn't controlling BP, an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide-like diuretic is usually preferred over going above 10 mg.\n\nPlease talk this over with your resident or attending before ordering. If they want 20 mg on purpose, tell me and I'll enter it with a note documenting that it's an intentional above-maximum dose. If you'd like one of the standard doses instead, tell me which and I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
